=[tomet-cst-parser]

==[tomet-cst-parser]

The lossless, resilient Concrete Syntax Tree parser for Tomet.

==[Architecture & Responsibilities]

- ("Deterministic Static Parser Boundary")
  - `tomet-cst-parser` is strictly a pure, side-effect-free, deterministic static parser.
It performs zero I/O, external file resolution, or dynamic code execution.
Given identical input text, it produces an identical lossless CST output.
- ("Nesting Limit")
  - Past [`MAX_DEPTH`] levels the CST keeps the text flat in a `TOO_DEEP` node, still
lossless.
- ("Zero AST Dependency")
  - `tomet-cst-parser` knows only the concrete syntax tree and lexical structure,
having zero dependency on AST representations or semantic lowering.

==[Module cst]

Resilient, lossless Concrete Syntax Tree (CST) parser for Tomet.

Preserves 100% of source characters including trivia (whitespace, newlines, comments).

This is the one reader of the grammar's shape -- where a block, an element, a
group starts and ends. `parse_document` is this tree lowered into the AST
(see `lower.rs`), so a grammar change is made here once. The parser itself
never fails: broken input still gets a tree, and the errors a document is
refused with are found when the tree is lowered.

```text
ROOT
  SECTION                    `=[ T ]` and everything up to the next section
    SECTION_HEADING          of the same or a shallower level (they nest)
    PARAGRAPH | LIST | BLOCK_ELEMENT | CODE_BLOCK | THEMATIC_BREAK | SECTION ...
```

An element (`BLOCK_ELEMENT`, `INLINE_ELEMENT`) is a `SIGIL` followed by
its groups -- `ARGS` `( )`, `VALUE_DATA` `{ }`, `CONTENT` `[ ]`, at most
one of each -- then any `CONNECT` (`:name(...)`).
`${ ... }` is an `INTERP_EXPR`. Inside `ARGS`/`VALUE_DATA` an entry is a
`MAP_ENTRY` (`key: value`) or a `SEQ_ITEM`, and nested groups are
`MAP_LITERAL`, `CONTENT` (a value written `[ ... ]` is blocks, as in the AST
parser; the list literal is gone) or (a call such as `list(...)`) `ARGS`.
`CONTENT` recurses into the same block grammar `ROOT`/`SECTION` use
(`SECTION`, `LIST`, `BLOCK_ELEMENT`, `CODE_BLOCK`, `THEMATIC_BREAK`,
`PARAGRAPH`), bounded by the group's own closing `]` instead of EOF or
a shallower section -- a block nested inside `[content]` (and its own
`#(id)`) is represented, not flattened into inline text.

A `CODE_BLOCK` is a fence of three backticks or more, or -- two backticks at the
start of a line -- a line raw: the lines that open that way, with no closing line.

Running text carries `EMPHASIS`, `STRONG`, `STRIKEOUT`, `CODE_SPAN` and
`AUTOLINK` nodes where the AST has an `em`, `strong`, `strikeout`,
`raw` or `link` element. What opens a span, and where an address ends, is
decided by the same functions in `inline.rs`, so the two cannot drift.
The tokenizer is context-free and emits tokens finely enough that delimiters
and structural boundaries are already token boundaries. The parser never cuts
tokens; it assembles compound structures (such as string literals in value
contexts and compound identifiers) where the grammar requires them.

A `|` run is `[content]` spelled without brackets, and so it is the same
node: a `CONTENT` that opens with a `PIPE` and has no closing `]`. It is an
element's content (`@quote| text`, or `|` on the line after the element), a
list item's (`-| text`), a heading's (`=| text`) or a `key: | prose` value.
It ends at the first line that does not repeat its marker in the same column
-- a blank line, or a `|` somewhere else, ends it too -- and the error for that
last case (a marker in a column that continues nothing) is lowering's, not the tree's. The `|` that begins each later
line is a `PIPE` token among the content's own, but the grammar reads it as
white space, so the blocks of a run are the blocks the bracketed form has.

===[Functions]

====[fn parse_cst]

Parse `src` into a lossless, resilient [`SyntaxNode`].

Guaranteed Invariant: `parse_cst(src).text().to_string() == src`.

====[fn parse_cst_value]

[`parse_cst`] for a source that is one value, as [`crate::parse_value`] reads
it: the entries of its map (or its one value) under a `ROOT`, with the
elements, `[content]` and `|` runs they embed as nodes.

===[Constants]

====[const MAX_DEPTH]

How deep constructs may nest: a `[content]` in an element in a `[content]`, a group in a group, an
emphasis in an emphasis, a list under a list, a section under a section. Past it the parser stops
reading structure and keeps the text flat in a `TOO_DEEP` node, so no recursion here, or in what
reads the tree, goes deeper than this (a deeper document used to abort the process with a stack
overflow).

==[Module inline]

CST delimiter scanning and autolink probes: the lexical probes that say
where a formatting delimiter (`*em*`, `_em_`, `**strong**`, `__strong__`,
`~~strikeout~~`) or an autolink begins and ends.

===[Structs]

====[struct DelimiterIndex]

Where a delimiter could close a span, and where a paragraph ends, found once for a text.

Whether an opener has a closer is then a lookup. It used to be a scan to the end of the paragraph
for every opener, so a long paragraph with many unmatched `*word`s took time quadratic in its
length.

===[Functions]

====[fn char_before]

====[fn is_boundary]

====[fn delimited_probe]

Whether `delim` opens a span at `cur`: it stands there, is not the middle
of a word (for `_`), has text right after it, and a closing `delim` follows
before the paragraph ends (`index` says).

====[fn autolink_scheme_at]

The length of the scheme (`https://`, `http://`, `mailto:`) an autolink
starts with at `cur`, if one starts there and not in the middle of a word.

====[fn autolink_end]

Where the autolink that starts at `start` ends, given that the address
ran up to `scanned_end` (the first whitespace or the end of its group):
without the punctuation and the unmatched closers that belong to the
sentence around it. `None` if nothing is left after the scheme.

===[Constants]

====[const DELIMITERS]

The inline delimiters and the element each one makes, longest first so
`**` is tried before `*`.
